/**
 * Myth — Do ravens attack humans?
 * MYTH·13
 * Primary keyword: do ravens attack humans
 * Secondary: raven attack, are ravens aggressive to humans, raven swooping
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Do Ravens Attack Humans? Aggression and Nest Defence';
const description = 'Ravens can be aggressive near nest sites during the breeding season, sometimes swooping at or striking people. This page examines documented raven-human aggression, its causes, and how common attacks actually are.';
const path        = '/myths/do-ravens-attack-humans/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',  url: '/' },
  { name: 'Myths', url: '/myths/' },
  { name: 'Do Ravens Attack Humans?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Have ravens ever attacked people?', a: 'Yes, ravens have attacked people, but serious attacks are rare and typically associated with specific circumstances: nest defence during the breeding season, defense of food caches, or ravens that have been habituated to humans and have lost fear. During the breeding season (February–May in most of the range), nesting pairs can be aggressive, swooping at and occasionally striking people who approach the nest. Injuries from such attacks are usually minor — a glancing blow or scratch — but can be startling.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens dangerous to humans?', a: 'Ravens are not generally dangerous to humans. A healthy, unhabituated wild raven will typically avoid people. The cases of raven aggression are almost always (1) nest defence by breeding pairs near the nest; (2) habituated or captive ravens that have lost fear and may bite in a food context; or (3) stress responses when ravens are handled or cornered. Ravens\' bills are powerful — capable of opening large carcasses — and can cause cuts if a bird bites in self-defence. But unprovoked attacks on humans in the wild are genuinely rare.' },
  { q: 'Why would a raven swoop at a person?', a: 'Swooping at a person is almost always a defensive behaviour, not an offensive one. The most common cause is nest proximity — if a person walks close to an active raven nest during the breeding season, one or both parents will swoop repeatedly to drive the intruder away. This is the same behaviour seen in many nesting birds, from magpies to red-winged blackbirds. The simplest response is to move away from the area; ravens rarely persist once the person leaves the nest zone.' },
];
---

