    </ol>
  </nav>

  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Myths vs Facts · MYTH·13</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Attack Humans? Aggression and Nest Defence</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="id-verdict verdict-partly-true" role="note" aria-label="Verdict">
    <strong>Verdict: Partly True</strong> — Ravens can and do act aggressively toward humans in specific circumstances (nest defence, habituation, handling). Serious, unprovoked attacks in the wild are genuinely rare; nesting-season swooping at intruders near the nest is documented and real.
  </div>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens can swoop at and occasionally strike humans who approach active nest sites during the breeding season. This defensive behaviour is real but context-specific — it is primarily nest defence, not random aggression. Unprovoked attacks on humans by wild ravens in non-nesting contexts are rare. Ravens are not generally dangerous to people.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>When Ravens Are Aggressive</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven aggression toward humans falls into identifiable categories:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nest defence:</strong> breeding pairs defend the nest and immediate surroundings (typically within 50–100 m) aggressively during the breeding season (February–June in most of the range). Both members of the pair participate. Behaviour ranges from alarm calling to close swooping passes to contact strikes on the head or back.</li>
      <li><strong>Food defence:</strong> ravens that have learned to associate humans with food (at campsites, landfills, tourist areas) may become bold, taking food from hands or bags. Such habituated birds may bite if resisted.</li>
      <li><strong>Captive or imprinted birds:</strong> ravens raised by humans ("imprinted" birds) may show aggression as social behaviour — testing the human's rank through biting and mocking attacks. This is a behavioural artefact of imprinting, not wild raven behaviour.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven Bill and Potential for Injury</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> A raven's bill is powerful — it can open the skin and muscle of large mammal carcasses. If a raven makes direct contact during a defensive swoop, it can cause cuts, bruising, or scratches, particularly on exposed scalp or hands. Such injuries, while painful, are not typically serious. Ravens do not pursue sustained grappling attacks as some large raptors do; a defensive pass or strike is brief and aimed at deterrence.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Practical Response</h2>
    <p>If a raven swoops at you, the most effective response is to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Move away from the area — the bird is almost certainly defending a nearby nest.</li>
      <li>Avoid reaching toward the bird or making threatening gestures, which will escalate the response.</li>
      <li>Do not attempt to handle the bird.</li>
      <li>Covering the head (hat, hood) provides protection against contact strikes if leaving the area takes time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Once away from the nest zone, ravens will typically cease defensive behaviour immediately.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
